WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.214
In this lesson we look at equations whose right side is not zero: for example a battery is connected to a circuit,

2
00:00:08.214 --> 00:00:10.951
or an external force acts on a spring.

3
00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:20.822
We split the general solution into two parts and find the particular solution from an almost ready template by looking at the right side.

4
00:00:21.072 --> 00:00:29.905
The basic structure: the general solution equals the general solution of the homogeneous equation plus one particular solution.

5
00:00:29.905 --> 00:00:36.304
The reason is linearity: the difference of two solutions satisfies the homogeneous equation.

6
00:00:36.304 --> 00:00:42.494
Initial conditions are always applied to the full sum, not to the homogeneous part alone.

7
00:00:42.744 --> 00:00:50.310
If the right side is a quasi-polynomial, built from an exponential, a polynomial, cosine and sine,

8
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:55.560
we attach to it the control number sigma equal to alpha plus i beta.

9
00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:03.280
If sigma is not a characteristic root, the particular solution has the same shape as the right side.

10
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.688
If sigma is a root of multiplicity s, we multiply the template by x to the power s.

11
00:01:09.688 --> 00:01:11.078
This is resonance.

12
00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:15.477
Example: y double prime plus y equals four sine x.

13
00:01:15.477 --> 00:01:22.696
This is a spring without friction whose forcing frequency equals its natural frequency.

14
00:01:22.696 --> 00:01:26.430
The characteristic roots are plus or minus i.

15
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.574
The control number sigma is i, a simple root, so s equals one.

16
00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:37.157
Template: x times, in brackets, A cosine x plus B sine x.

17
00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:43.893
Note that although only a sine is on the right, the cosine is taken too.

18
00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:53.342
Differentiating twice and substituting, the left side becomes minus two A sine x plus two B cosine x.

19
00:01:53.592 --> 00:01:59.754
Match the coefficients: minus two A equals four and two B equals zero.

20
00:01:59.754 --> 00:02:06.972
So A is minus two, B is zero, and the particular solution is minus two x cosine x.

21
00:02:06.972 --> 00:02:13.838
The general solution is C one cosine x plus C two sine x minus two x cosine x.

22
00:02:14.088 --> 00:02:19.226
The graph shows the particular solution minus two x cosine x.

23
00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:22.258
Its amplitude grows linearly with x.

24
00:02:22.258 --> 00:02:33.039
This is what resonance looks like: the force pushes at just the right moment every time and the oscillation grows without bound.

25
00:02:33.039 --> 00:02:37.334
That is why real bridges and machines have dampers.

26
00:02:37.584 --> 00:02:47.096
Your turn: in which form should you look for a particular solution of y double prime plus four y equals cosine two x?

27
00:02:47.096 --> 00:02:52.787
Find the control number and check whether it is a characteristic root.

28
00:02:52.787 --> 00:02:55.958
Pause the video and choose your answer.
